From: MediaBox
Subject: dec: ask the decoder for eight-bit frames, and stop asserting on the rest

This plugin has exactly one capture format, NV12, because that is the one
Chromium's V4L2 decoder can import. It never tells MPP so. A ten-bit stream
therefore decodes into MPP_FMT_YUV420SP_10BIT -- NV15 -- and the plugin meets
it with

    assert(dec->video_info.mpp_format == MPP_FMT_YUV420SP);

AV1 Main 10 is not a corner case on a television: it is what YouTube serves at
2160p, and the assert runs inside the browser's GPU process, so the answer to a
ten-bit video would have been the display going away rather than one video
failing.

Two changes, both small.

The decoder is asked for eight-bit output. MPP_DEC_SET_OUTPUT_FORMAT is the
existing control for this and the hardware honours it in its own output stage:
the RK3588 AV1 block still reconstructs the stream at ten bits, and what lands
in the DMA-BUF is NV12. Nothing is converted on the CPU and no frame crosses
the bus a second time. The picture is eight-bit, which is where Chromium was
going to end up anyway -- its renderable formats on this board are NV12 and
AR24 -- so the depth is lost at the same place either way, and this way it is
lost inside the decoder instead of in a copy.

The assert becomes a refusal. With the control above it should be unreachable;
it is kept because an assert that can take the television down is the wrong
shape for this code however sure we are, and a stream that finds a way past
the control should cost a decoder, not a display.
--- a/src/libv4l-rkmpp-dec.c
+++ b/src/libv4l-rkmpp-dec.c
@@ -272,6 +272,20 @@
 		return;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * A frame format this plugin cannot describe is a reason to stop, not
+	 * to abort. With the output format asked for in streamon this is not
+	 * reachable; it is here so that a stream which finds a way past that
+	 * loses a decoder and not the whole browser.
+	 */
+	if (video_info.mpp_format != MPP_FMT_YUV420SP) {
+		LOGE("unsupported mpp format(%d), refusing the stream\n",
+		     video_info.mpp_format);
+
+		LEAVE();
+		return;
+	}
+
 	dec->video_info = video_info;
 	dec->video_info.dirty = true;
 	dec->video_info.event = dec->event_subscribed;
@@ -294,7 +308,6 @@
 	ctx->capture.format.plane_fmt[0].sizeimage =
 		dec->video_info.size;
 
-	assert(dec->video_info.mpp_format == MPP_FMT_YUV420SP);
 	ctx->capture.format.pixelformat = V4L2_PIX_FMT_NV12;
 
 	LEAVE();
@@ -522,6 +535,7 @@
 	struct rkmpp_context *ctx = dec->ctx;
 	struct rkmpp_buf_queue *queue;
 	MppPollType poll_type;
+	MppFrameFormat out_fmt;
 	uint32_t split;
 	MPP_RET ret;
 
@@ -579,6 +593,20 @@
 		goto err_destroy_mpp;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Eight bits out, whatever came in. Chromium imports NV12 here and
+	 * nothing else, and without this a ten-bit stream -- AV1 Main 10, the
+	 * one a television actually meets -- arrives as NV15 and is refused
+	 * above. The hardware still decodes at ten bits; only its output stage
+	 * is narrowed, so no frame is converted on the CPU to get here.
+	 */
+	out_fmt = MPP_FMT_YUV420SP;
+	ret = ctx->mpi->control(ctx->mpp, MPP_DEC_SET_OUTPUT_FORMAT, &out_fmt);
+	if (ret != MPP_OK) {
+		LOGE("failed to set mpp output format\n");
+		goto err_destroy_mpp;
+	}
+
 	/* Enable timeout mode to avoid hang during get_frame */
 	poll_type = RKMPP_DEC_POLL_TIMEOUT_MS;
 	ret = ctx->mpi->control(ctx->mpp, MPP_SET_OUTPUT_TIMEOUT,
